Declarative vs Scripted Pipelines in Jenkins

the two syntaxes Jenkins supports: Declarative and Scripted. Both achieve the same goal (defining pipelines), but they differ in structure, readability, and flexibility.

1. Declarative Pipeline
Modern, opinionated syntax introduced to simplify Pipeline-as-Code
Starts with a top-level pipeline {} block
Stages and steps are predefined & structured
Validation built into Jenkins → easier for beginners
Ideal for most CI/CD use cases

pipeline {
 agent any
 stages {
  stage('Build') { steps { sh 'npm install' } }
  stage('Test') { steps { sh 'npm test' } }
  stage('Deploy'){ steps { sh 'pm2 restart app' } }
 }
}

2. Scripted Pipeline
Original, fully Groovy-based syntax
Offers maximum flexibility & programmatic control
No strict structure → you write pipeline logic as Groovy scripts
Requires deeper Groovy knowledge → better for complex conditions or loops

node {
 stage('Build') { sh 'npm install' }
 stage('Test') { sh 'npm test' }
 stage('Deploy'){ sh 'pm2 restart app' }
}

Takeaways
Declarative → clean, safe, and beginner-friendly (recommended for most teams)
Scripted → power & flexibility (use when complex logic is unavoidable)
Teams often start Declarative, then mix in Scripted steps when needed.
